<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> An electric dipole of dipole moment p is placed in a uniform electric field E. The maximum torque acting on the dipole is given by:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) p E</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">p</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">E</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Zero</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) p &times; E</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The drift velocity v<sub>d</sub> of free electrons in a conductor varies with the applied electric field E as:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) v<sub>d</sub> &prop; E</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) v<sub>d</sub> &prop; <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">E</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) v<sub>d</sub> &prop; E<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) v<sub>d</sub> is independent of E</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A charged particle moves with velocity v in a uniform magnetic field B such that v is perpendicular to B. The work done by the magnetic force on the charge is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Maximum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Minimum</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Zero</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Dependent on the charge</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The magnetic susceptibility &chi; of a diamagnetic material is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Small and positive</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Large and positive</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Small and negative</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Zero</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The self-inductance L of a long solenoid of length l, area of cross-section A, and total number of turns N is proportional to:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) N<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">N</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">N<sup>2</sup></span></span></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> In a series LCR circuit at resonance, the phase angle between current and voltage is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 0<sup>\circ</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 45<sup>\circ</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 90<sup>\circ</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 180<sup>\circ</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Which of the following electromagnetic waves has the shortest wavelength?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Microwaves</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Ultraviolet rays</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Gamma rays</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) X-rays</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A convex lens of focal length f is immersed in water. Its focal length will:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Decrease</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Increase</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Remain unchanged</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Become negative</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> In Young's double-slit experiment, if the distance between the two slits is halved, the fringe width becomes:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Half</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Double</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Four times</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) One-fourth</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through a potential difference V is inversely proportional to:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) V</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">V</span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) V<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">V</span></span></span></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> The radius of the inner-most electron orbit of a Hydrogen atom is 5.3 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> m. What is the radius of the orbit in the second excited state (n = 3)?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1.59 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> m</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 2.12 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> m</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 4.77 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> m</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 1.06 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> m</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> When a p-n junction diode is reverse biased, the width of the depletion layer:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Decreases</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Increases</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Remains constant</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) First decreases then increases</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Directions: In the following questions, a statement of Assertion (A) is followed by a statement of Reason (R). Choose the correct option:
(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).
(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).
(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.
(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> Assertion (A): Electrostatic field lines do not form closed loops.
Reason (R): Electrostatic field is conservative in nature.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Assertion (A): Above Curie temperature, a ferromagnetic substance behaves as a paramagnetic substance.
Reason (R): On heating, the exchange coupling between domain spins breaks down.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> Assertion (A): Light waves can be polarized while sound waves cannot be polarized.
Reason (R): Light waves are transverse in nature, whereas sound waves are longitudinal in nature.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q16.</strong> Assertion (A): The binding energy per nucleon is maximum for <sub>56</sub>Fe nucleus.
Reason (R): <sub>56</sub>Fe nucleus is the most stable nucleus.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q17.</strong> Two point charges +q and -2q are placed at a distance r apart in air. Find the position of the point on the line joining the two charges where the electric potential is zero.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q18.</strong> State Kirchhoff's rules for an electrical network. Mention the physical principle on which each rule is based.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q19.</strong> Draw a ray diagram to show the formation of an image by a astronomical telescope in normal adjustment. Write the expression for its magnifying power.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q20.</strong> Draw a plot showing the variation of stopping potential with frequency of incident radiation for two different photosensitive materials having work functions W<sub>1</sub> and W<sub>2</sub> (W<sub>1</sub> > W<sub>2</sub>). What does the slope of the graph represent?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q21.</strong> Distinguish between n-type and p-type semiconductors on the basis of energy band diagrams at absolute zero temperature.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions with appropriate derivations/explanations.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q22.</strong> Using Gauss's law, derive an expression for the electric field intensity due to an infinitely long straight uniformly charged wire of linear charge density &lambda;. Plot a graph showing the variation of electric field with distance r.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q23.</strong> Derive an expression for the force per unit length between two long, straight parallel current-carrying conductors placed in vacuum. Hence, define one ampere.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q24.</strong> A series LCR circuit containing resistance R = 20  &Omega;, inductance L = 1.5 H, and capacitance C = 35  &mu;F is connected to a variable frequency 220 V AC supply. Calculate:
(a) The resonant frequency of the circuit.
(b) The impedance and current at resonance.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Explain the concept of displacement current introduced by Maxwell. Write the modified Ampere's circuital law equation.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q26.</strong> Derive Lens Maker's Formula for a double convex lens of radii of curvature R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> placed in a medium of refractive index n<sub>1</sub>, with the material of the lens having refractive index n<sub>2</sub>.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q27.</strong> State Bohr's postulates for the hydrogen atom model. Derive the expression for the total energy of an electron in the n<sup>th</sup> stationary orbit.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q28.</strong> Draw the circuit diagram of a full-wave rectifier using p-n junction diodes. Briefly explain its working principle and draw the input and output waveforms.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Long Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions in detail. Internal choice is provided within each question.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q29.</strong> Answer either Part A or Part B:

Part A:
(i) Derive an expression for the electric field at a point on the equatorial line of an electric dipole of dipole moment p.
(ii) A parallel plate capacitor with air between the plates has a capacitance of 8 pF. What will be the capacitance if the distance between the plates is reduced by half and the space between them is filled with a substance of dielectric constant K = 6?

OR

Part B:
(i) State the principle of a parallel plate capacitor. Derive an expression for its capacitance when a dielectric slab of thickness t (t < d) is introduced between its plates.
(ii) Two capacitors of capacitances 2 &mu;F and 4 &mu;F are connected in series across a 120 V source. Find the potential difference across each capacitor.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q30.</strong> Answer either Part A or Part B:

Part A:
(i) State Huygens' principle. Using Huygens' wave theory, verify the laws of refraction of light at a plane interface separating two media.
(ii) In Young's double slit experiment, monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm illuminates the slits. The distance between the slits is 1 mm and the screen is placed 1 m away. Calculate the distance of the 3rd bright fringe from the central maximum.

OR

Part B:
(i) Draw a labeled ray diagram of a compound microscope forming an image at the least distance of distinct vision. Derive an expression for its magnifying power.
(ii) A small telescope has an objective lens of focal length 144 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 6.0 cm. What is the magnifying power of the telescope in normal adjustment? What is the separation between objective and eyepiece?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q31.</strong> Answer either Part A or Part B:

Part A:
(i) State the principle and working of a Moving Coil Galvanometer with a neat labeled diagram. Explain the function of a radial magnetic field in it.
(ii) How can a galvanometer be converted into an ammeter? Obtain the required formula for the shunt resistance.

OR

Part B:
(i) State the principle of an AC Generator. Draw a neat labeled diagram and derive the expression for the instantaneous induced EMF generated in a rotating coil of N turns.
(ii) A rectangular coil of 200 turns and area 0.1  m<sup>2</sup> rotates at 600 rpm in a uniform magnetic field of 0.05 T. Find the maximum EMF induced in the coil.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section F: Case Study Based Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Read the following passages and answer the questions that follow.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q32.</strong> CASE STUDY 1: Total Internal Reflection and Optical Fibres

When a ray of light traveling from a denser medium to a rarer medium is incident at an angle greater than the critical angle for the pair of media, the ray is completely reflected back into the denser medium. This phenomenon is called Total Internal Reflection (TIR). Optical fibres consist of a core surrounded by cladding, where the refractive index of the core material is greater than that of the cladding material. Signal transmission through optical fibres relies on repeated total internal reflections, maintaining signal strength over long distances with negligible loss.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>State the two essential conditions for Total Internal Reflection to occur.</li><li>Write the mathematical relation connecting the critical angle (i<sub>c</sub>) and the refractive index (n) of the denser medium relative to the rarer medium.</li><li>Why is the refractive index of the core chosen to be greater than that of the cladding in an optical fibre?</li><li>Calculate the critical angle for a glass-air interface if the refractive index of glass is 1.5.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q33.</strong> CASE STUDY 2: Semiconductor p-n Junction Diode

A p-n junction is formed when a p-type semiconductor is joined with an n-type semiconductor. Immediately upon formation, diffusion of majority carriers creates a depletion region devoid of free charge carriers, generating a built-in potential barrier across the junction. Under forward bias, the applied voltage opposes the potential barrier, narrowing the depletion region and allowing significant current to flow. Under reverse bias, the applied voltage aids the potential barrier, widening the depletion region and allowing only a tiny reverse saturation current.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>What causes the formation of the depletion region across a p-n junction?</li><li>How does the width of the depletion layer change when the junction is forward biased versus reverse biased?</li><li>What are the primary majority charge carriers in p-type and n-type semiconductors respectively?</li><li>Draw the V-I characteristics curve of a silicon p-n junction diode under both forward and reverse bias.</li></ol>
</body>
</html>